#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078142 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078527 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cc2167e-d318-54d5-8b67-47afbc65f530" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Mapping Diagram Is a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6d57991d-4cdb-546b-80c5-4df14f1b385d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1096" :question/problem "Is the following rule a function?\n\n![](images/91/91cddcd85f3b6a9efd0fc898ef2004e9e8310593554142de8c94bc4fd1a0ab40.png)" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if *every* input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\nIn particular, a rule is *not* a function in the following cases:\n\n- An input value is not assigned to an output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with no arrow from it):\n\n![](images/bb/bb53865d210d631d78e5ce48aa96ecff99da8995936a085c789c3471ffb9e6a0.png)\n\n- An input value is assigned to more than one output value. For example, the following situation is *not allowed* for functions (an input value with more than one arrow from it):\n\n![](images/18/18831dc73e561faa69748fed111eb7e1024d35849fb6624594ae08d8c35301ee.png)\n\nIn this particular example, the rule *is* a function because *each* input value is assigned *precisely one* output value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078381 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b869d240-38f8-52c8-bb87-110d7ac616b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Table Is a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "279a2ac1-2577-5ec3-8eb4-8496650c6b54" :question/math-academy-id "e-9905" :question/problem "Which missing $xy$-pair would make the following rule a function?\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-1$ | ? | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $4$ | ? | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $7$ |\n\n1. $(1,2)$\n2. $(7,3)$\n3. $(-1,5)$" :question/worked-solution "A rule is a function if and only if every input is mapped to *precisely one* output.\n\n- If we substitute the $xy$-pair $(1,2)$ into our table, we get a function.**$x$**$-3$$-1$$\\color{blue}1$$3$$5$$7$$9$$y$$0$$4$$2$$0$$2$$1$$7$Notice that all values in the first row are *distinct* (i.e., different). Every input value is assigned to *precisely one* output value.\n- None of the other $xy$-pairs gives rules that are functions. So, for example, substituting the $xy$-pair $(-1,5)$ gives a rule that is *not* a function. **$x$**$-3$$\\color{red}-1$$\\color{red}-1$$3$$5$$7$$9$$y$$0$$4$$5$$0$$2$$1$$7$Notice that the first row contains the value $\\color{red}x=-1$ twice. The input value $-1$ is assigned to *more than one* output value ($4$ and $5$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b869d240-38f8-52c8-bb87-110d7ac616b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Rule Given by a Table Is a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d18e8bbd-0f71-5800-add5-92b4ebc5cf11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076047} {:answer/id #uuid "d33382cf-a277-5ba0-a66f-5f94ff3071d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076048} {:answer/id #uuid "2c290509-977d-5bec-97eb-7a76d83405c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076049} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3aacdf-7192-5507-ac00-772fcb5d061d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076050} {:answer/id #uuid "48447a27-666a-58f3-ba33-701af8ebc7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076051}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4afc5af1-157b-5384-b7d7-f5bca76286d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076046}] :question/id #uuid "b22f15dc-cbde-5d31-8d71-51f8fb6a253c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37248" :question/problem "Consider a rule between $x$ and $y$ given by the table below.\n\n| **$x$** | $12$ | $9$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $12$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The rule is a function\n2. The rule is **not** a function\n3. The rule maps the input $x = 15$ to the output $y = 12$"}]}
